# Line-coverage floor, in percent. Read by .github/workflows/ci.yml and by Tools/Tasks.cs, so the
# gate a developer runs locally is the gate CI runs. One value, one file, no second copy to drift.
#
# Set from measurement, never invented. A threshold above the real number fails on day one and gets
# deleted; a threshold far below it lets coverage rot unnoticed, which is the same as having none.
# The rule here is: a few points under the measured value, so an honest refactor has room but a
# real regression trips it.
#
# Measured history — `dotnet run Tools/Tasks.cs coverage` prints the current value:
#   2026-08-05   83.80%  (2261/2698)  715 tests
#   2026-08-05   86.53%  (2750/3178)  974 tests
#   2026-08-05   87.84%  (3562/4055)  1187 tests
#   2026-08-10   85.34%  (9186/10764) 3089 tests
#   2026-09-04   90.46%  (7874/8704)  3259 tests
#
# The two lines below are measured by a different engine and are NOT comparable to the five above.
# Those were taken by coverlet; these by the code-coverage extension that Microsoft.Testing.Platform
# collects with, which counts sequence points rather than coverlet's lines. Same code, same tests,
# same run: 6215 measurable lines in Release where coverlet saw 8704, and a total four points
# higher. Do not read the jump as coverage that was written; nothing was.
#
# The configuration is part of the measurement and has to be stated with it. An optimised build
# merges and drops sequence points, so Release measures fewer lines than Debug and scores higher
# over them. CI runs Release; `dotnet run Tools/Tasks.cs coverage` runs Debug unless told
# otherwise, which is the figure a developer sees first and therefore the binding one.
#   2026-09-04   94.11%  (5849/6215)  3264 tests   extension, Release  (what CI enforces)
#   2026-09-04   92.82%  (7029/7573)  3264 tests   extension, Debug    (the lower of the two)
#
# The weakest assembly is AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Email at 63.87%; then
# AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity at 85.96% and AppTemplate.Api at 86.11%. Every other one is
# above the floor, AppTemplate.Domain at 100.00% and AppTemplate.Application at 98.90% highest
# among them. Read from the Debug row, which is the lower one. The
# total is what the gate enforces, so a large new project with no tests can pull it down even while
# every other one improves. Read this list as of the last dated measurement above and no later: it
# is written by hand, and nothing makes it fall out of step loudly.
#
# THE 68.22% ABOVE WAS MEASURED WITHOUT A SINGLE IDENTITY UNIT TEST IN IT. coverage.runsettings named
# GeneratedCodeAttribute in ExcludeByAttribute, and coverlet 10.0.1 drops a whole assembly from the
# report when one of its classes is a source-generated partial — which SecurityEventLog is, being a
# LoggerMessage class. So the report from AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity.UnitTests held no
# AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity package at all: 187 passing tests, scored zero, and the 68.22%
# came entirely from the two integration suites. The exclusion is now written as an ExcludeByFile
# glob over obj/, which covers the same generated code — every other assembly's line counts are
# identical to the digit — and does not lose the module.
#
# What that is worth, measured on this machine, where Docker is absent so both integration suites
# contribute nothing and neither figure is comparable to the four above:
#   before   59.96%  (6472/10793)   AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity absent from the report
#   after    68.08%  (7326/10761)   AppTemplate.Infrastructure.Identity 58.13% (854/1469)
#   then     69.33%  (7461/10761)   AppTemplate.Worker 57.22% -> 81.28% (321 -> 456 lines)
# Eight points from tests that were already written and already passing, and one more from the two
# worker files nothing had ever executed: Common/Observability/WorkerObservabilityExtensions.cs and
# Common/Outbound/OutboundHttpExtensions.cs, both at 0 of 70 and 0 of 67 lines. What is left in that
# assembly is Program.cs at 0/28, which a unit test cannot reach, and the uncovered halves of the
# three loops.
#
# The Docker-present total has not been re-measured since — Docker is unavailable on the machine
# these three lines were taken on, so both integration suites contributed nothing to all three — and
# the 85.34% below is therefore still the last honest figure for the gate, now an understatement of
# unknown size. Re-measure on a machine with Docker before touching the floor either way.
#
# WHY THE FLOOR MOVED, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A LOOSENING. Changing the collector re-based every
# number in this file, and a floor is only worth what its margin is: 85 against a coverlet-measured
# 90.46% was 5.46 points, while 85 against an extension-measured 92.82% is 7.82 points — the same
# figure protecting less. 88 restores the margin the rule above asks for and is therefore a
# tightening of the gate, not a relaxation of it. It is set against the Debug row because that is
# the lower of the two, so a local run cannot fail a gate that CI would pass — a floor that
# depends on which configuration you happened to build is not a floor. That leaves 4.82 points of
# margin locally and 6.11 in CI. Measured first, rule read second, and not the other way round.
#
# Measured with Docker present, so both integration suites contributed. Re-measure that way before
# touching this number again: without a daemon the two suites run nothing and the figure is not
# comparable to any line above.
88
